  "body": "## Summary\r\n\r\n### What is the goal of this PR?\r\n\r\nCJK text needs more leading than Latin text, and the current line-spacing scale tops out at values tuned for Latin. Latin typesetting conventionally defaults to ~120% line-height; CJK glyphs are dense squares with no ascender/descender rhythm to visually separate lines, so the same leading reads cramped. From Peter Biľak's [*Typesetting principles of Chinese, Japanese, and Korean (CJK) text*](https://www.typotheque.com/articles/typesetting-cjk-text) (Typotheque):\r\n\r\n> For example, in a 10-point font, the default leading would typically be 12 points. This works well for most Latin fonts, with adjustments only needed for fonts with unusually large x-heights. For CJK (Chinese, Japanese and Korean) text, which tends to have a higher density of information per character, a leading value of around 1.7 improves both readability and aesthetic balance.\r\n\r\nWith SD-card CJK fonts now supported (v1.5.0), readers of Korean/Chinese/Japanese books hit this wall: even the Wide setting is visibly tight for Hangul and Han text. This PR adds one more step, **Extra Wide**, so CJK readers can move toward comfortable leading — while staying useful as a plain accessibility/preference option for Latin readers too.\r\n\r\n### What changes are included?\r\n\r\nEach font family's existing increment progression is continued rather than inventing a new scale (values are the internal line-compression factor applied to the font's built-in line height, not absolute leading):\r\n\r\n* Noto Serif / SD-card fonts: 0.95 / 1.0 / 1.1 / **1.2** (existing 0.1 steps at the top of the scale)\r\n* Noto Sans: 0.90 / 0.95 / 1.0 / **1.05** (uniform 0.05 steps, matching that family's existing increments)\r\n\r\nConcretely:\r\n\r\n* One new enum value `EXTRA_WIDE`, inserted before `LINE_COMPRESSION_COUNT` so stored-settings range validation keeps working; older firmware settings load unchanged.\r\n* One entry in the declarative `SettingsList`, which drives both the on-device settings UI and the web settings page.\r\n* `STR_EXTRA_WIDE` labels for **all 32 languages** (translations provided for every locale; native-speaker corrections welcome).\r\n* No cache-format change: the compression factor is already part of the section layout cache header, so selecting the new step invalidates and re-lays-out automatically.\r\n\r\n## Preview\r\n<img width=\"1080\" height=\"840\" alt=\"hp_wide_vs_extra_wide\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/77a57e70-6790-4a26-a4f8-46d77b32d8a5\" />\r\n\r\n## Scope Check\r\n\r\n- [x] I have read SCOPE.md and ROADMAP.md.\r\n- [x] This PR is **not** a new built-in theme.\r\n- [x] This PR is **not** a new external network connector.\r\n- [x] This PR is **not** an interactive app, writing tool, RSS/news/browser, media playback, or PDF feature.\r\n- [x] This extends an existing reader typography setting (line spacing) by one step; it adds no new subsystem.\r\n- [x] This PR does not touch `freeink-sdk/`, `lib/hal/`, bootloader, OTA, or recovery code.\r\n\r\n## Additional Context\r\n\r\n* **Memory / flash impact:** effectively none — one enum value, one settings-list entry, and one short string per language in the flash-resident string tables.\r\n* **Settings compatibility:** values 0–2 keep their meaning; 3 is new. `LINE_COMPRESSION_COUNT` moves from 3 to 4, so validation accepts the new value and nothing previously stored becomes invalid.\r\n* **Hardware testing:** X3, SD-card font (Pretendard), Korean EPUBs: Extra Wide lays out correctly, section cache regenerates on switch, and the setting round-trips through both the device UI and the web settings page.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### AI Usage\r\n\r\nDid you use AI tools to help write this code? _**YES**_ — the patch and translations were done with Claude Code with Fable 5; hardware testing was manual. Non-English `STR_EXTRA_WIDE` labels are AI-provided translations phrased to match each locale's existing Tight/Normal/Wide labels.\r\n",
